h_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which two structural properties are upheld by a linear transformation?","options":["Division by vectors and taking square roots.","Vector addition and scalar multiplication.","Only translation by a constant.","Only multiplication of coordinates."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7085c4cc1776fd345dad7821"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is a linear transformation?","options":["$f(x,y,z) = x-y+2z+1$","$f(x,y) = 1$","$f(x,y) = x+y$","$f(x,y) = x^2+y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0eaf84951c4e3cafc8f4275"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is not a linear transformation?","options":["$f(x,y) = x+y$","$f(x,y,z) = x-y+2z+1$","$f(x_1,x_2) = 2x_1-3x_2$","$f(x) = 5x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"671261f36f26c78548d25ff6"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}$, what is the general expression given?","options":["$f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n) = a_1x_1+a_2x_2+\\cdots+a_nx_n$","$f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n) = a_1x_1^2+\\cdots+a_nx_n^2$","$f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n) = a_0+a_1x_1+\\cdots+a_nx_n$","$f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n) = a_1/x_1+\\cdots+a_n/x_n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"833e194d2424ee5c433fa647"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is stated about the graph of a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["It is always a circle centred at the origin.","It is a disconnected finite set.","It is a hyperplane that cannot pass through the origin.","It is a hyperplane of dimension $n$ that goes through the origin."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b6fd97349eb25019351ea683"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x,y) = 2x-3y$. Which matrix row represents this linear transformation?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\ -3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-3&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}x&y\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed977428d13417c0fa61a88d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For a linear transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}$, what is $\\boldsymbol{f}(2\\boldsymbol{x})$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})+2$","$2\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})^2$","$\\boldsymbol{0}$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"abd38fa19ce1a2709a40dbfe"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $f(x,y,z) = x-y+2z+1$ not linear?","options":["It has three input variables.","It has coefficients with different signs.","It maps into $\\mathbb{R}$.","It has a constant term, so it is not homogeneous."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"26226992bf909d8dab70944b"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a nonzero linear map $f:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}$, why are level sets $f(x)=c$ parallel hyperplanes rather than unrelated surfaces?","options":["Changing $c$ shifts the same kernel-shaped hyperplane without changing its normal direction.","Linearity forces every level set to pass through the origin, no matter which values are selected.","A scalar-valued linear map can have only one level set.","Level sets of linear maps are always spheres."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6240c9f8bf408c9d0732f7b2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is linear, which equality must hold for any $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{y})$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})+\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{y})$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})-\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{y})$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{y}) = \\boldsymbol{0}$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bf2d1e12805d58663d33e716"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_6c595a2b_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A map has the form $\\boldsymbol{g}(\\boldsymbol{x}) = \\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}+\\boldsymbol{b}$ with $\\boldsymbol{b} \\neq \\boldsymbol{0}$. Which issue prevents it from being linear under this definition?","options":["Its matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has entries.","Its domain is $\\mathbb{R}^n$.","It is not represented purely as $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$.","Its codomain is $\\mathbb{R}^k$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bf1c524da7d6e918b1e2c659"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How can a linear transformation $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^k$ be represented?","options":["$\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol x)=\\boldsymbol A+\\boldsymbol x^2$ always.","$\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol x)=\\boldsymbol A\\boldsymbol x$ for some real $k\\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol A$.","$\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol x)=c$ for one fixed scalar only.","$\\boldsymbol f$ cannot be represented by a matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"843977d64181fd56687e4193"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which two operations are preserved by a linear transformation?","options":["Vector addition and set union.","Scalar multiplication and taking boundaries.","Vector addition and scalar multiplication.","Integration and compactness."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b5c4144376f9973ab1e00828"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the general form of a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$a_1x_1+a_2x_2+\\cdots+a_nx_n$","$a_0+a_1x_1^2+\\cdots+a_nx_n^2$","$x_1x_2\\cdots x_n$","$\\sin(x_1)+x_2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f8c683e0db4bf5e386aeeec0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function $f:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is linear?","options":["$f(x,y)=3x-2y$","$f(x,y)=x^2+y$","$f(x,y)=xy$","$f(x,y)=x+y+1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"410ae1ebc77653bb241b64a6"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol x=(1,1)^\\intercal$. What is $\\boldsymbol A\\boldsymbol x$?","options":["$(1,3)^\\intercal$","$(2,0)^\\intercal$","$(3,3)^\\intercal$","$(3,0)^\\intercal$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8111e3ea9b311f62df70e61f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function fails to be linear because of a constant term?","options":["$f(x,y)=x-y$","$f(x,y)=2x$","$f(x,y)=0$","$f(x,y)=x+y+1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"57e1a2d8c352bcacdfe27e8c"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must a linear transformation satisfy $\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol 0)=\\boldsymbol 0$?","options":["Because every function sends zero to zero.","Because the codomain contains only zero.","Because matrices cannot contain nonzero entries.","Because $\\boldsymbol f(0\\boldsymbol x)=0\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol x)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1465fca93ae6ce7a8f0ed8d5"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_linear_transformation_58aa1c4e_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x,y)=2x-y$. Which equality shows preservation of addition?","options":["$f((x_1,y_1)+(x_2,y_2))=f(x_1,y_1)+f(x_2,y_2)$","$f(x,y)=f(y,x)$","$f(x,y)+1=f(x+1,y+1)$, a shift property rather than linearity.","$f(x,y)=f(x,y)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"071729f4e8d6000cb3df908e"}]}}